<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Élysée Accords</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1949</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Elysee Accords · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Élysée Accords were agreements signed on 8 March 1949 by the French President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-auriol">Vincent Auriol</a> and the former emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a>. They recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, including Cochinchina, as an Associated State within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a> under Bảo Đại, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> kept control of defence and foreign affairs. Intended as a non-communist alternative to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>, the resulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a> won little popular support.</p></div>
